<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#druid">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Druid</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Druides, Druí</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A druid was a member of the learned class of the ancient Celtic peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, described by Greek and Roman writers as priest, teacher, judge and keeper of religious and legal tradition. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>, the druids were one of the two honoured classes of Gaul beside the horsemen; they conducted sacrifices, judged nearly all disputes, were exempt from tax and military service, and met yearly in the land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnutes">Carnutes</a>. Because they wrote nothing down, almost all that is known of them comes from outsiders. They declined after the Roman conquest and were the object of prohibitions under Augustus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/druid.jpg" alt="Druid" width="330" height="411" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernard de Montfaucon · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Two_Druids.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word druid comes through French and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> from the Gaulish druides, and most scholars derive it from a Proto-Celtic compound read as oak-knower or as a priest with strong insight. Classical authors placed druids only in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. The earliest notices, of the 4th and 3rd centuries BCE, treat them as barbarian philosophers akin to the Persian magi. Because the druids committed nothing of their teaching to writing and cannot be securely identified in the archaeological record, nearly everything known about them comes from outside observers, much of it descended from the lost ethnography of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/posidonius">Posidonius</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Diodorus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, drawing on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/posidonius">Posidonius</a>, describe druids arbitrating public and private disputes and even halting armies drawn up for battle. According to Caesar they had a single head who held office for life, and their pupils might remain in instruction for up to twenty years, memorising a great body of verse. The doctrine most often remarked on was the immortality of the soul.</p><p>Caesar and the Posidonian authors agree that no sacrifice took place without a druid. Diodorus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> describe divination from a human victim, and Caesar reports victims burned inside a huge wickerwork image. Scholars weigh these reports differently: some note that the barbarity had propaganda value for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, while Bruce Lincoln holds that they go back to Greek sources older than any such motive. Excavations in pre-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Roman Gaul</a> show animal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a>, though the priests who officiated are nowhere named.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Augustus forbade the druidic religion to Roman citizens, a decree of the Senate under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> was directed against druids, diviners and physicians, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suetonius">Suetonius</a> says <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> abolished their religion. In Ireland, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> never reached, medieval literature preserves many druids, but these accounts are generally read as literary constructs of Christian authors.</p><p>From the 16th century druids became a screen for reinterpretation. John Aubrey attributed Stonehenge and Avebury to them, though the monuments are millennia older than the Iron Age Celts. In 1792 Edward Williams, known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iolo-morganwg">Iolo Morganwg</a>, invented the Gorsedd Beirdd Ynys Prydain, whose ritual survives in the National Eisteddfod. The Order of Bards, Ovates and Druids was founded in 1964, but modern Druidry has no genuine continuity with the ancient priesthood.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Druid” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Peter Berresford Ellis likened them to the Brahmin caste, Anne Ross to tribal shamans, and Ronald Hutton held that “we can know virtually nothing of certainty about the ancient <b>Druids</b>”.</blockquote><a href="/religion/celtic-religion">Celtic religion · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Society was ranked, with nobles, a priestly class of <b>Druids</b>, merchants, artisans and farmers, and status came partly from birth and partly from achievement.</blockquote><a href="/culture/la-tene-culture">La Tène culture · Society</a></li></ul></section>
